cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  ct-dosemc-core: patient-specific absorbed organ dose in CT by open Monte Carlo
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it, and cite MC-GPU for the transport
  engine and SpekPy for the spectra it depends on.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yamamoto
    given-names: Shuji
    email: yamamoto@lisit.jp
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071'
    affiliation: Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo 150-0044, Japan
repository-code: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ct-dosemc-core
url: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ct-dosemc-core
abstract: >-
  A Python package that computes patient-specific absorbed organ dose in
  computed tomography, in mGy, by Monte-Carlo photon transport through the
  patient's own segmented and density-mapped anatomy. Transport uses MC-GPU
  (public domain); x-ray spectra come from SpekPy (BSD-3); material
  cross-sections are generated locally from the photon subset of the PENELOPE
  2006 database rather than transcribed; organ boundaries come from
  TotalSegmentator run at inference. Because every dose coefficient is computed
  rather than looked up, the pipeline carries none of the licensing exposure of
  published organ-dose coefficient tables, and no such table is redistributed.
  Transport is verified against Case 4 of AAPM TG-195 with a replica-measured
  uncertainty and a reference code chosen before any comparison was run, and
  the dose scale is anchored to the CTDI dosimetry phantom of IEC 60601-2-44, a
  physical standard, before any patient dose is computed. Every reported value
  carries its formula, inputs, assumptions and Monte-Carlo statistical
  uncertainty, and the test suite re-derives the quoted figures from the recorded
  inputs. No imaging is redistributed; public series are identified by Series
  Instance UID, collection and licence so they can be re-fetched.
keywords:
  - computed tomography
  - Monte Carlo
  - organ dose
  - absorbed dose
  - CTDI
  - MC-GPU
  - SpekPy
  - TotalSegmentator
  - DICOM
  - reproducibility
  - dose index
  - TG-195
  - cross-validation
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0
# Zenodo takes this as the record's publication date and a DOI cannot be
# re-dated afterwards, so it is the day the tag is cut, not the day work began.
date-released: "2026-10-21"
# The *concept* DOI goes here once the first release is minted; it resolves to
# the latest version, which is what a citation of the project should point at.
# A result should cite the version DOI of the release it was produced with.
# doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXXX
